WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:06.220
Just like what we have seen in the previous sessions,

00:00:06.220 --> 00:00:10.200
we play with these five digits. And here's our question.

00:00:10.580 --> 00:00:13.480
Take a moment and read it well!

00:00:13.760 --> 00:00:15.900
Using these five digits,

00:00:15.960 --> 00:00:20.760
how many three-digit numbers greater than 200 can be formed?

00:00:21.080 --> 00:00:23.020
Let's read it again.

00:00:23.040 --> 00:00:26.620
Three-digit numbers greater than 200.

00:00:26.940 --> 00:00:31.360
And again we have two cases. When repetition is not allowed

00:00:31.360 --> 00:00:33.100
and when it's allowed.

00:00:33.440 --> 00:00:38.060
Let's understand the first case! We have to form three-digit numbers,

00:00:38.060 --> 00:00:41.200
so we make three slots for each of the digits.

00:00:41.480 --> 00:00:43.020
Read the question again.

00:00:43.160 --> 00:00:45.080
Do we have a condition here?

00:00:45.280 --> 00:00:48.620
Yes, the condition is that the three-digit number

00:00:48.660 --> 00:00:50.860
must be greater than 200.

00:00:51.220 --> 00:00:54.500
And we have seen before that if we have a condition,

00:00:54.520 --> 00:00:56.580
we always start with it.

00:00:56.860 --> 00:00:59.920
It means, we start with the hundredths place.

00:01:00.220 --> 00:01:05.440
This is a golden rule. If you're given a condition always start with it.

00:01:05.860 --> 00:01:08.360
Now ask yourself one simple question.

00:01:08.660 --> 00:01:11.380
If the number has to be greater than 200,

00:01:11.420 --> 00:01:14.840
then in how many ways can the hundreds place be filled?

00:01:15.180 --> 00:01:19.340
Don't forget! We can use only one of these five digits.

00:01:19.760 --> 00:01:21.740
Think, it's easy!

00:01:21.940 --> 00:01:24.920
If the number has to be greater than 200,

00:01:24.920 --> 00:01:28.580
then the hundredths place should have a digit greater than two.

00:01:28.860 --> 00:01:33.280
So we can fill the hundredths place with a three or a four or a five.

00:01:33.620 --> 00:01:35.380
In three ways correct?

00:01:35.680 --> 00:01:38.880
This is a very common mistake that students do.

00:01:39.180 --> 00:01:40.420
It's incorrect!

00:01:41.000 --> 00:01:42.860
The hundredths place can be filled with a

00:01:42.860 --> 00:01:46.140
two or a three or a four or a five.

00:01:46.440 --> 00:01:49.760
Even 213 is greater than 200.

00:01:49.960 --> 00:01:53.620
245 is also greater than 200.

00:01:53.860 --> 00:01:57.060
T he only number that will not be greater than 200,

00:01:57.060 --> 00:02:00.740
when 2 is in the hundredths place, is 200.

00:02:00.940 --> 00:02:04.160
But here, zero is not one of the five digits.

00:02:04.320 --> 00:02:08.620
So we don't have to worry about that special case of 200.

00:02:08.620 --> 00:02:12.980
There are four ways in which the hundredths place can be filled.

00:02:13.200 --> 00:02:17.080
With a 2 or a 3, or a 4 or a 5.

00:02:17.400 --> 00:02:21.040
Not a one as the number has to be greater than 200.

00:02:21.340 --> 00:02:23.340
Now we move on to the next slot.

00:02:23.700 --> 00:02:27.560
Assuming we have filled the hundredths place with one of these four digits.

00:02:27.640 --> 00:02:30.740
In how many ways can the tenths place be filled?

00:02:30.780 --> 00:02:32.500
Think carefully.

00:02:32.540 --> 00:02:34.480
One digit is used here.

00:02:34.480 --> 00:02:37.520
In how many ways can we fill the second slot?

00:02:37.740 --> 00:02:40.780
As one digit is gone four remain.

00:02:40.820 --> 00:02:45.220
Simple! The second slot can be filled in four ways.

00:02:45.280 --> 00:02:47.000
If we use a four here,

00:02:47.020 --> 00:02:51.440
then one among one, two, three, and five can be used here.

00:02:51.580 --> 00:02:53.280
If we use a two here,

00:02:53.300 --> 00:02:57.240
then one among one, three, four, and five can be used here.

00:02:57.320 --> 00:03:00.420
So having used one digit in the hundredths place,

00:03:00.420 --> 00:03:03.580
the tenths place can be filled in four ways.

00:03:03.860 --> 00:03:07.900
Now having used up two digits for the hundredths and the tenths place,

00:03:07.900 --> 00:03:10.960
in how many ways can the unit's digit be filled?

00:03:11.220 --> 00:03:13.220
As two digits are used,

00:03:13.220 --> 00:03:15.480
obviously three would be left.

00:03:15.560 --> 00:03:17.600
It can be filled in three ways.

00:03:17.760 --> 00:03:21.520
We are filling the first and the second and the third slot.

00:03:21.620 --> 00:03:23.680
So we multiply these numbers.

00:03:23.860 --> 00:03:27.780
The total number of ways would be four times four times three.

00:03:27.780 --> 00:03:30.140
It equals forty eight ways.

00:03:30.320 --> 00:03:32.120
And what is this 48?

00:03:32.360 --> 00:03:33.520
It is the number of

00:03:33.520 --> 00:03:36.860
different three-digit numbers greater than 200,

00:03:36.900 --> 00:03:40.200
that can be formed using these five digits.

00:03:40.440 --> 00:03:43.200
But when repetition is not allowed.

00:03:43.560 --> 00:03:46.860
Will the answer change if repetition is allowed?

00:03:47.100 --> 00:03:49.100
Let's move to a new page.

00:03:52.040 --> 00:03:54.540
We try to answer the second part here.

00:03:54.900 --> 00:03:56.860
Using these five digits,

00:03:56.860 --> 00:03:59.600
how many three-digit numbers greater than 200

00:03:59.600 --> 00:04:03.440
can be formed, if repetition is allowed?

00:04:03.740 --> 00:04:07.100
We make three slots to represent the three digits.

00:04:07.340 --> 00:04:10.960
In how many ways can we fill the hundredths place?

00:04:11.260 --> 00:04:14.000
As the number has to be greater than 200,

00:04:14.000 --> 00:04:18.180
we can fill it with a two or a three or a four or a five.

00:04:18.320 --> 00:04:19.720
Four ways.

00:04:20.000 --> 00:04:23.240
Okay good! Now answer this question

00:04:23.360 --> 00:04:25.640
Having used up one digit here,

00:04:25.640 --> 00:04:28.560
in how many ways can we fill the second slot?

00:04:35.860 --> 00:04:38.320
So after having used up one digit here,

00:04:38.320 --> 00:04:42.580
does it mean that there are just four ways in which you can fill up the slot?

00:04:42.740 --> 00:04:44.360
No, it does not.

00:04:44.380 --> 00:04:48.540
Repetition is allowed if we use a three here.

00:04:48.540 --> 00:04:51.140
We can use it again in the tenths place.

00:04:51.260 --> 00:04:55.240
The number of ways in which the tenths and the unit's place can be filled

00:04:55.240 --> 00:04:59.780
is independent of the condition as repetition is allowed.

00:05:00.160 --> 00:05:02.800
The second slot or the tenths place can be filled with

00:05:02.800 --> 00:05:05.540
any one of the five digits we have.

00:05:05.780 --> 00:05:07.960
It can be filled in five ways.

00:05:08.320 --> 00:05:13.200
And with the same logic, even the unit's place can be filled in five ways.

00:05:13.540 --> 00:05:15.940
Why four ways for the first slot?

00:05:16.140 --> 00:05:18.680
Because we cannot fill it with a one.

00:05:18.960 --> 00:05:21.700
The number has to be greater than 200.

00:05:22.020 --> 00:05:24.880
We multiply these numbers as we are filling the first,

00:05:24.900 --> 00:05:27.780
and the second and the third slot.

00:05:28.260 --> 00:05:33.020
Four times five is twenty and twenty times five is 100.

00:05:33.340 --> 00:05:35.860
One hundred ways is the answer!

00:05:36.080 --> 00:05:40.260
We can form one hundred three-digit numbers greater than 200,

00:05:40.320 --> 00:05:44.200
using these five digits when repetition is allowed.

00:05:44.520 --> 00:05:47.940
Do we have to start with the condition when repetition is allowed?

00:05:47.940 --> 00:05:52.980
Nope! When repetition is allowed we can start off with any of the slots.

00:05:53.240 --> 00:05:58.220
We will get the same answer.

